      "writtenOn": "2021-06-24T02:25:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Calculating exact sizes and adjusting offsets is not improving performance.\n\nIf the Flashchip is using one of the drivers with the JEDEC spec and containing the 5 byte buff then the efficiency will be near 100%. If it is following a driver that doesn\u0027t have the the final packet will be 5 bytes smaller.\n(33+(59/64))/34 \u003d 99.8% efficiency\n\nOn the other hand, JEDEC drivers with this bug will attempt to write 35 packets of data with the final packet only having 5 bytes of useful data.\n(34+(5/64))/35 \u003d 97.4% efficiency\n\nRight now the bug and the quick and dirty correction for the bug are both located in the Flashrom project, that\u0027s a self contained location. If there\u0027s a different but similar bug which requires 6 bytes of offset or the bug in the JEDEC implementation is fixed, we\u0027re not going to end up with random interfaces adding or subtracting a magic number because there\u0027s an idea that it\u0027ll save a few milliseconds during each flash on specific chips.\n\nSame argument can be made for the USB_SPI_MAX_WRITE_COUNT where it should really just be START_PAYLOAD_SIZE + N*CONT_PAYLOAD_SIZE. The flash drivers are aware of data alignment and their protocol limitations, in addition there are multiple flash protocols. You\u0027ve hardcoded an optimization for one type of driver interface when a dozen exist and more will be added later on.",
      "revId": "846394f0cd3e0da15c0e66ac603c887bd92f3133",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
